{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nWhen a ticket records no `file_impact`, attestation currency falls back to invalidating on ANY code-SHA change — a deliberate fail-safe, but measured to over-trigger badly: 307 of 1,457 full-LLM plan re-reviews (21%) ran on byte-identical material fingerprints, 171 of them triggered by base-SHA drift alone (analysis ticket b902-18af-14ed-4da1). Allowing drift without re-review and requiring file_impact universally are both rejected; the approved design scopes the fail-safe instead.\n\n## Plan (approved components a + b)\n\nThis design EXTENDS the shipped ADR-0002 content-addressed currency machinery — it does not add a parallel trigger. ADR 0002 already scopes invalidation to per-path content hashes of file_impact ∪ cited files; the gap is only the NO-file-impact case, which today falls back to invalidate-on-any-SHA-change. It is also disjoint from the Pass-3 DRIFT floor (bug 5e40, `drift_floor.py`), which governs finding-level novelty WITHIN a review; this ticket governs attestation currency BETWEEN reviews.\n\n### (a) Record the review's read-set INSIDE the signed material\n\n**Source.** The agentic passes already observe which repository files they open: `rebar.llm.usage_log.run_shape` emits `distinct_fetches` — a list of `{\"tool\", \"target\"}` records where `fetch_target` (`usage_log.py:282-310`) returns the `path` argument for `read_file` and `list_directory`, and the `query`/`pattern`/`regex` STRING for `search_files`.\n\n**Normalization protocol (exact, deterministic, order-independent).** A new module `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/read_set.py` reduces `distinct_fetches` to the signed read-set by applying, in order:\n\n1. **Tool filter.** Keep ONLY entries with `tool == \"read_file\"`. `search_files` targets are query strings, not paths, and are dropped. `list_directory` targets are directories, which `_hash_file` cannot hash meaningfully; they are dropped rather than expanded (a directory listing is not content the review grounded on, and expanding it would import unread files into the dependency set).\n2. **Root resolution.** Resolve each target against the review's hash root — `_hash_basis(repo_root)`, i.e. the pinned-SHA snapshot root during an attested review, else the checkout. A relative target is joined to that root; an absolute target is used as-is.\n3. **Containment.** `os.path.realpath` the result and DROP it if it does not lie under the realpath of the hash root. This discards `../`-escapes and absolute paths outside the repo.\n4. **Repo-relative POSIX form.** Re-express the survivor as a `/`-separated path relative to the hash root (`os.path.relpath` then `PurePath.as_posix()`), which also strips a leading `./` and collapses `.`/`..` segments.\n5. **Existence filter.** DROP any path that is not an existing regular file under the hash root. A mistyped or speculative read contributed no content to the review, so baking `_ABSENT_HASH` for it would make a later unrelated file creation invalidate the attestation — the exact over-invalidation this ticket removes.\n6. **Dedupe + sort.** Collect into a set and emit sorted, so two spellings of one file (`./src/x.py` and `src/x.py`) collapse to one entry and the manifest is reproducible.\n\n**Telemetry plumbing (today `distinct_fetches` does NOT reach the signing site).** It is stripped twice: `sizing.usage_record` (`sizing.py:289-307`) keeps only `criteria`/`requests`/token fields, and `_attach_plan_review_metrics` (`workflow/plan_review_recovery.py:97-182`) reads only `requests` off the agent step. Three additive edits carry it through, each preserving the existing keys:\n- `sizing.usage_record` passes `distinct_fetches` through when present;\n- `pass1.aggregate_usage` unions the per-call `distinct_fetches` lists (dedup on `(tool, target)`);\n- `_attach_plan_review_metrics` walks `rec.steps`, unions every step's `_usage[\"distinct_fetches\"]`, normalizes via `read_set.py`, and writes `verdict[\"coverage\"][\"read_set\"]` (a sorted list) plus `verdict[\"coverage\"][\"read_set_recorded\"] = True`.\n\n`read_set_recorded` is the fail-safe discriminator: `True` means telemetry was successfully collected (the list may still be empty); absent/False means no agentic pass ran or collection failed, and the scoping in (b) is NOT applied.\n\n**Signed record.** `build_manifest` gains sorted `read-path: <path>` lines plus one `read-set: <count>` line, emitted only when `read_set_recorded` is true. Because the signature binds the whole manifest, a modified or truncated read-set fails signature verification, which resolves to `verdict=\"unsigned\"` in `compute_validity` — the fail-safe (invalidate). An unsigned copy may also ride in the sidecar for observability; currency evaluation reads only the signed manifest.\n\n### (b) Feed the read-set into the ADR-0002 dependency set\n\nFor a ticket that records **no** `file_impact` and whose manifest carries a recorded read-set, `dependency_hashes` composes the dependency set as **read-set ∪ plan-cited paths ∪ the expanded blast-radius entries**, and hashes it with the SAME `{path: sha256}` machinery ADR 0002 already ships. `compute_validity`'s per-path re-hash loop is unchanged: it still iterates the concrete recorded entries and compares digests. A ticket that DOES record `file_impact` keeps today's set exactly (file_impact ∪ cited ∪ inherited child impact) — the read-set is recorded for observability but changes no currency decision.\n\n**Blast-radius entries and glob expansion.** The named entries are:\n`src/rebar/llm/criteria/**`, `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_routing.json`, `.rebar/criteria_routing.json`, `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/*.md`, `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gates/*.yaml`, and `rebar.toml`.\n(`src/rebar/llm/criteria/**` is deliberate and is NOT a mis-transcription of the rubric paths: that package holds the threshold resolver and overlay-merge core, which change a review's blocking/advisory outcome even when no rubric text moves. The rubric content paths are named separately.)\n\nBecause ADR-0002's currency check iterates the concrete recorded paths, a glob is handled in two complementary ways at ATTESTATION time:\n\n- **Expansion.** Every glob is expanded (deterministic sorted `glob.glob(..., recursive=True)` over the hash root, regular files only) and each match is recorded as an **ordinary** `dep <sha256> <path>` entry. Content drift in any member is therefore caught by the unchanged per-path check, with a precise per-file drift message.\n- **Membership digest.** Additionally, the glob PATTERN itself is recorded as a dep entry whose digest is the sha256 of the sorted, newline-joined list of its matched repo-relative paths. This closes the addition/deletion blind spot: a reviewer prompt added after signing changes the membership digest even though it has no baked per-file hash, and a deleted member changes both the membership digest and (via `_ABSENT_HASH`) its own entry.\n\nBoth sides of the comparison route through ONE shared helper, `read_set.hash_dep_entry(entry, base=...)`, which dispatches on whether the entry contains a glob metacharacter (`*`, `?`, `[`): a glob entry yields the membership digest, a plain path delegates to the existing `_hash_file`. Signing (`dependency_hashes`) and the claim-gate re-check (`compute_validity`, `_rehash`) both call it, so the two bases cannot diverge — the same single-boundary discipline ADR 0002 §\"Shared ref-resolution boundary\" already applies to the ref.\n\n**Fail-safes (never weaker than the status quo).** Each of these falls back to today's invalidate-on-any-change (empty dependency set → whole-HEAD freshness, `file_scope != \"none\"`):\n- the manifest carries no recorded read-set (a pre-change attestation, or a review where no agentic pass ran / telemetry collection failed);\n- signature verification fails, including a tampered or truncated read-set (`verdict=\"unsigned\"`);\n- hash-basis resolution or glob expansion raises — `dependency_hashes` catches, logs, and omits the read-set-derived scoping.\n\nA review that read no code and cites no code (a genuinely no-code ticket) is guarded only by the blast-radius entries and stops re-triggering on unrelated code motion — the original complaint — while a review that DID consult code is invalidated exactly when consulted, cited, or blast-radius material moves.\n\n### (c) Report the basis\n\n`build_manifest` records one authenticated `currency-basis: file_impact | read-set | fail-safe` line, chosen at sign time (`file_impact` when the ticket declared paths, `read-set` when the no-file-impact scoping applied, `fail-safe` when neither produced a dependency set). `plan_review_status` (`attest_gate.py:163-214`, no LLM / no network) reads it off the signed manifest and adds it to its returned dict; a legacy manifest with no such line is derived as `file_impact` when dep lines exist, else `fail-safe`. The `--status` text renderer in `_cli/_llm_commands.py` prints it on the `current=` line; JSON mode carries the same key.\n\n### (d) Document the extended contract\n\nADR 0002 gains an addendum section recording the signed read-set field, the no-file-impact dependency-set composition rule, and the glob expansion + membership-digest handling, so the one-way-door manifest SHAPE contract stays documented in one place.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The signed attestation manifest records the agentic passes' distinct repository read paths (normalized per the six-step protocol above) for reviews where any agentic pass ran, and is absent when no read-set was recorded; signature verification fails on a modified read-set. Verify: `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" python -m pytest tests/unit/test_plan_review_attest_drift.py -k \"read_set\" -x -q` passes with new tests pinning the field, the normalization cases (search_files/list_directory dropped, `./` prefix collapsed, outside-repo dropped, nonexistent dropped, duplicates deduped), and the tamper case.\n- [ ] With no file_impact recorded and a moved SHA, currency evaluation reuses the ADR-0002 content-hash check over read-set ∪ cited paths ∪ the expanded blast-radius entries; an untouched dependency set leaves the attestation current, a touched path invalidates, and a file ADDED under a blast-radius glob invalidates via the membership digest. Verify: `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" python -m pytest tests/unit/test_plan_review_attest_drift.py -k \"no_file_impact or blast_radius\" -x -q`.\n- [ ] Tampered/unverifiable read-set, pre-change attestations lacking the field, and hash/expansion failure each fall back to invalidate-on-any-change (fail-safe preserved). Verify: `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" python -m pytest tests/unit/test_plan_review_attest_drift.py -k \"fallback\" -x -q`.\n- [ ] `rebar review-plan <id> --status` reports which basis (file_impact | read-set | fail-safe) currency was decided on, in both text and JSON output. Verify: `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" python -m pytest tests/unit/test_plan_review_attest_drift.py -k \"currency_basis\" -x -q`.\n- [ ] ADR 0002 documents the read-set field, the no-file-impact composition rule, and the glob expansion + membership digest. Verify: `grep -n \"read-set\" docs/adr/0002-code-drift-invalidation.md` returns the addendum."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786496253040184001,"uuid":"83884a1a-39f2-4996-9ac5-f3349666b877"}